As a Premier League team, Leicester City started in the third round, playing Championship team [[Stoke City F.C.|Stoke City]] away at the [[Bet365 Stadium]].  The home side's [[Sam Vokes]] struck his shot over the crossbar before [[James Justin]] opened the scoring for Leicester in the 34th minute.  [[Marc Albrighton]] doubled the lead fourteen minutes into the second half before strikes from [[Ayoze Pérez]] and [[Harvey Barnes]] secured a 4–0 victory.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55574481|title=Foxes power past Stoke in FA Cup|date=9 January 2021|access-date=18 April 2021|publisher=[[BBC Sport]] | archive-date=24 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210124041258/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55574481|url-status=live}}</ref> In the fourth round, they played Championship side [[Brentford F.C.|Brentford]] at the [[Brentford Community Stadium]].  [[Mads Bech Sørensen]] put the home side ahead after seven minutes to give them a 1–0 half-time lead.  Within a minute of the restart, [[Cengiz Ünder]] scored from a [[James Maddison]] pass to level the score.  [[Youri Tielemans]] was then fouled in the Brentford penalty area and scored the resulting penalty to make it 2–1 to Leicester City.  Maddison then scored from a rebound after Brentford's goalkeeper [[Luke Daniels]] failed to hold on to Barnes' shot, to give Leicester City a 3–1 win.<ref>{{cite web |title=Leicester overcome Brentford scare |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=22 January 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55770058 |access-date=18 April 2021 |archive-date=28 February 2021 | first= Stephan | last= Shemilt |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210228140059/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55770058 |url-status=live }}</ref> 

Leicester's fifth round home match was at home at the [[King Power Stadium]] against fellow Premier League team [[Brighton & Hove Albion F.C.|Brighton & Hove Albion]]. After a goalless first half, both Ünder and Brighton's [[Andi Zeqiri]] had goals disallowed before [[Kelechi Iheanacho]] headed in a Tielemans cross four minutes into second-half stoppage time to give his side a 1–0 victory.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55953166|title=Iheanacho winner sends Leicester through|date=10 February 2021|access-date=18 April 2021|publisher=[[BBC Sport]]| archive-date=21 February 2021| first= Shamoon | last= Hafez | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210221155757/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55953166|url-status=live}}</ref> Leicester played fellow Premier League side Manchester United at home in the quarter-final.  Iheanacho capitalised on a poor backpass by [[Fred (footballer, born 1993)|Fred]] to score past [[Dean Henderson]] in the 24th minute.  [[Mason Greenwood]] equalised for Manchester United seven minutes before half time but Tielemans restored Leicester's one-goal lead with a low shot in the 52nd minute. Iheanacho scored his second and Leicester's third twelve minutes before the end of the match to secure a 3–1 win.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/56415040|title=Leicester see off Man Utd to reach semis|date=21 March 2021|access-date=18 April 2021|publisher=[[BBC Sport]] | first = Phil | last= McNulty | authorlink = Phil McNulty| archive-date=17 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210417085407/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/56415040|url-status=live}}</ref>  Wembley Stadium hosted the semi-final as a neutral venue where Leicester City faced [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]], another Premier League team, in front of 4,000 spectators as part of a pilot scheme with coronavirus restrictions easing.  No goals were scored in the first half but Iheanacho's 55th minute strike secured a 1–0 win for Leicester City and qualification for their first FA Cup final since 1969.<ref name=lc>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/56725449|title=Leicester reach first FA Cup final since 1969|date=18 April 2021|access-date=18 April 2021| first = Phil | last= McNulty | authorlink = Phil McNulty | publisher=[[BBC Sport]]|archive-date=18 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210418081150/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/56725449|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Background==
This was [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]]'s fifteenth [[FA Cup Final]] appearance and their fourth in the last five seasons, having missed out in [[2019 FA Cup final|2019]]. However, they won only one of those four, beating [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] 1–0 in [[2018 FA Cup final|2018]]. They had lost 2020's final to [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]].<ref>{{cite web |first=Phil |last=McNulty |authorlink=Phil McNulty |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53546100 |title=FA Cup final 2020: Arsenal 2–1 Chelsea |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=1 August 2020 |access-date=17 April 2021 |archive-date=1 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200801202716/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53546100 |url-status=live }}</ref> Chelsea had won eight finals, seven of those occurred since [[1997 FA Cup final|1997]]. [[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]] appeared in four FA Cup Finals prior to 2021, losing them all; their most recent appearance was in [[1969 FA Cup final|1969]], when they were defeated 1&ndash;0 by [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]].<ref name=thefa>{{Cite web |url=https://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/stories/fa-cup-finals |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |title=FA Cup Finals – the winner's list |access-date=26 April 2021 |archive-date=10 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210410202646/https://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/stories/fa-cup-finals |url-status=live }}</ref>
In the clubs' 118 previous meetings, Chelsea won 57, Leicester City won 27 and the remaining 34 were drawn.  Leicester City had never beaten Chelsea in their seven previous FA Cup ties, although two had gone to a replay.<ref>{{cite web |title=Chelsea football club: record v Leicester City |url=https://www.11v11.com/teams/chelsea/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Leicester%20City/ | website=11v11 |access-date=15 May 2021 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |archive-date=14 October 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191014011631/https://www.11v11.com/teams/chelsea/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Leicester%20City/ |url-status=live }}</ref>  Their most recent meeting was in the [[2020–21 Premier League]] on 19 January 2021 where Leicester won 2–0 with goals from [[Wilfred Ndidi]] and Maddison.<ref>{{cite web |first=Phil |last=McNulty |authorlink=Phil McNulty |title=Leicester City 2–0 Chelsea: Leicester win to go top of Premier League |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55627849 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=19 January 2021 |access-date=15 May 2021 |archive-date=16 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210516015542/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55627849 |url-status=live }}</ref>  This was the first FA Cup Final since [[2013 FA Cup final|2013]] held before the Premier League season ended.<ref name="talkSPORT" /> With two league games remaining, Leicester were in third place, two points ahead of Chelsea in fourth.<ref>{{Cite episode|title=MOTD Live: FA Cup final|series=The FA Cup|network=[[BBC]]|station=[[BBC One HD]]|date=15 May 2021|minutes=5}}</ref> Leicester went into the final as the only prior [[List of English football champions|league champions of England]] not to have won the FA Cup.<ref>{{cite web|first1=Conor|last1=Pope|first2=Mark|last2=White|title=Leicester win their first FA Cup, live! Follow the Foxes win over Chelsea in the FA Cup Final 2021|url=https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/live/chelsea-vs-leicester-fa-cup-final-2021|work=[[FourFourTwo]]|date=15 May 2021|access-date=16 May 2021|quote=...{{nbsp}}and are the only team to have won the league and not won the FA Cup.|archive-date=15 May 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515174944/https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/live/chelsea-vs-leicester-fa-cup-final-2021|url-status=live}}</ref> This was also the first cup final Leicester participated in since the [[2000 Football League Cup Final]].<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.fchd.info/LEICESTC.HTM | website = Football Club History Database | access-date = 22 May 2021 | title = Leicester City | archive-date = 31 October 2020 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20201031103831/http://www.fchd.info/LEICESTC.HTM | url-status = live }}</ref>

[[Michael Oliver (referee)|Michael Oliver]] ([[Durham County Football Association|Durham]]) was the [[Referee (association football)|referee]], [[assistant referee (association football)|assisted]] by Stuart Burt and Simon Bennett. [[Stuart Attwell]] was the [[fourth official]], and Dan Cook the [[reserve assistant referee]]. [[Chris Kavanagh (referee)|Chris Kavanagh]] was the [[video assistant referee]] (VAR) and [[Sian Massey-Ellis]], acting as assistant VAR, became the first female referee involved in an FA Cup final.<ref name="referees">{{cite news |last=Veevers |first=Nicholas |url=https://www.thefa.com/news/2021/apr/20/michael-oliver-to-referee-emirates-fa-cup-final-20210420 |title=The lineup of match officials for the 2021 Emirates FA Cup Final has been selected |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |date=20 April 2021 |access-date=20 April 2021 |archive-date=20 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210420143146/https://www.thefa.com/news/2021/apr/20/michael-oliver-to-referee-emirates-fa-cup-final-20210420 |url-status=live }}</ref>

For their team selections, Chelsea dropped Abraham and brought in Ziyech. Leicester started [[Jonny Evans]] and had their captain [[Wes Morgan]] on the bench.<ref>{{Cite web |first=Gary |last=Rose |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/55503216 |title=Leicester win FA Cup after late VAR drama – player, manager & pundit reaction |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=15 May 2021 |access-date=20 May 2021 |archive-date=20 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210520082240/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/55503216 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Pre-match==
===Ticketing and attendance===
Due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic in the United Kingdom]], crowds at football matches in England had not exceeded 8,000 since March 2020.<ref name=lc /><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/fa-cup-final-covid-wembley-fans-b1816932.html|title=FA Cup final could see 10,000 fans at Wembley Stadium|date=14 March 2021|work=[[The Independent]]|access-date=17 April 2021|archive-date=17 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210417180532/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/fa-cup-final-covid-wembley-fans-b1816932.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.itv.com/news/granada/2021-04-25/a-cup-final-with-a-crowd-fans-watch-man-city-take-on-tottenham-at-wembley |title=A Cup final with a crowd: Fans watch Man City take on Tottenham at Wembley |publisher=[[ITV (TV network)|ITV]] |date=26 April 2021 |access-date=26 April 2021 |archive-date=26 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210426130811/https://www.itv.com/news/granada/2021-04-25/a-cup-final-with-a-crowd-fans-watch-man-city-take-on-tottenham-at-wembley |url-status=live }}</ref> [[The Football Association]] planned for the FA Cup Final to be part of a pilot scheme to allow fans to attend.<ref name=ss>{{Cite web|url=https://www.skysports.com/football/news/34651/12244148/fa-cup-final-among-events-to-be-used-as-pilot-for-large-scale-return-of-spectators|title=FA Cup final could see 20,000 fans at Wembley as pilot for large-scale return of spectators|publisher=[[Sky Sports]] |access-date=17 April 2021|archive-date=17 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210417180532/https://www.skysports.com/football/news/34651/12244148/fa-cup-final-among-events-to-be-used-as-pilot-for-large-scale-return-of-spectators|url-status=live}}</ref> The [[English Football League]] had delayed the [[2021 EFL Cup Final]], hoping to admit supporters under the same scheme.<ref name=ss /> 

On 14 March 2021, it was announced that up to 20,000 fans would be able to attend the final after it was selected for the pilot.<ref name=t>{{Cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/politics/2021/03/13/fa-cup-final-world-snooker-championships-will-kickstart-return/|title=Wembley Stadium to admit 20,000 fans to FA Cup final in cautious restart for spectator sport|first=Christopher|last=Hope|date=13 March 2021|access-date=17 April 2021|work=[[The Daily Telegraph]]|archive-date=17 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210417143109/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/politics/2021/03/13/fa-cup-final-world-snooker-championships-will-kickstart-return/|url-status=live}}</ref> This also came as Wembley was due to host several matches, including the final, of [[UEFA Euro 2020]] in the summer.<ref name=t /> Initial government plans required fans to provide evidence of their COVID vaccine status.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-56634176|title=Covid: Passports showing vaccine status would be time-limited, says minister|date=4 April 2021|access-date=17 April 2021|publisher=[[BBC News]]|archive-date=18 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210418020946/https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-56634176|url-status=live}}</ref> Following opposition, the government announced fans would not need vaccine passports but would still have to provide evidence of a recent negative COVID-19 [[lateral flow test]] in order to enter the stadium. After the event fans were requested to take a [[Polymerase chain reaction|PCR]] test to track whether the event caused any spread of COVID-19.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://inews.co.uk/news/politics/fans-show-negative-covid-tests-not-vaccine-passports-fa-cup-final-960065|title=Fans have to show negative Covid tests – not 'vaccine passports' – to get into FA Cup final|date=16 April 2021|work=[[i (newspaper)|i]]|access-date=17 April 2021|archive-date=16 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210416154642/https://inews.co.uk/news/politics/fans-show-negative-covid-tests-not-vaccine-passports-fa-cup-final-960065|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Broadcasting===
[[File:Gary Lineker (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright|alt=Gary Lineker|Former Leicester City player [[Gary Lineker]] ''(pictured in 2010)'' presented the BBC One coverage of the final.]]

The match was televised live in the United Kingdom on [[BBC One]] and [[BT Sport|BT Sport 1]]. BBC One's coverage was presented by former Leicester City player [[Gary Lineker]] alongside former [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]] forward [[Alan Shearer]], former Arsenal forward and two-time FA Cup winner [[Ian Wright]], and former Chelsea defender [[Ashley Cole]], with commentary from [[Guy Mowbray]] and former [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] midfielder [[Jermaine Jenas]]. BBC coverage of the final was watched by 9.1 million people in the United Kingdom, making it the most watched match of the season.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/57134944 |title=FA Cup final on BBC becomes the most-watched game of the season so far |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=16 May 2021 |access-date=19 May 2021 |archive-date=19 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210519080216/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/57134944 |url-status=live }}</ref> [[BBC Sport]] presenter [[Dan Walker (broadcaster)|Dan Walker]] presented the preview to the final on [[Football Focus]] with former [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]] player [[Dion Dublin]] and former [[Arsenal W.F.C.|Arsenal Women]]'s player [[Alex Scott (footballer, born 1984)|Alex Scott]].<ref>{{Cite news|title=FA Cup final: How to watch and follow Chelsea v Leicester on the BBC|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/57073294|publisher=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=15 May 2021|archive-date=15 May 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515105600/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/57073294|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=FA Cup final: Fans drown out TV interviews in raucous Wembley celebrations |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/57130885 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=15 May 2021 |access-date=15 May 2021 |archive-date=15 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515191240/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/57130885 |url-status=live }}</ref> BT Sport's broadcast was presented by [[Jake Humphrey]], former Chelsea midfielder [[Joe Cole]], former Leicester City forward [[Emile Heskey]], and former players [[Rio Ferdinand]] and [[Michael Owen]]. [[Ian Darke]] was BT's main commentator, joined by former Leicester midfielder [[Robbie Savage]] and former Chelsea player-manager [[Glenn Hoddle]]. Former referee [[Peter Walton (referee)|Peter Walton]] provided commentary on the match officiating.<ref>{{cite web |title=Emirates FA Cup Final 2020/21: Why BT Sport is THE best place to watch Chelsea vs Leicester |url=https://www.bt.com/sport/football/fa-cup/chelsea-leicester-fa-cup-final-best-channel-to-watch-live |publisher=[[BT Group|BT]] |date=10 May 2021 |access-date=15 May 2021 |archive-date=14 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210514213728/https://www.bt.com/sport/football/fa-cup/chelsea-leicester-fa-cup-final-best-channel-to-watch-live |url-status=live }}</ref> Under the terms of the broadcasting deal they struck with the FA in 2013,<ref>{{cite news |first=Owen |last=Gibson |title=BBC and BT Sport to share FA Cup TV rights |url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2013/jul/17/bbc-bt-sport-fa-cup-tv-rights |work=[[The Guardian]] |publisher=Guardian News & Media |date=17 July 2013 |access-date=15 May 2021 |archive-date=23 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190523180515/https://www.theguardian.com/media/2013/jul/17/bbc-bt-sport-fa-cup-tv-rights |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Mark |last=Sweney |title=FA Cup to stay on BBC and BT Sport until 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2016/apr/12/fa-cup-bbc-bt-sport-gary-lineker-womens-football |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=12 April 2016 |access-date=15 May 2021 |archive-date=15 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515205731/https://www.theguardian.com/media/2016/apr/12/fa-cup-bbc-bt-sport-gary-lineker-womens-football |url-status=live }}</ref> it was the last time BT Sport showed the FA Cup final, having lost the rights for the 2021–2025 broadcasting cycle to [[ITV (network)|ITV]] in 2019.<ref>{{cite web |title=FA Cup to be shown exclusively on free-to-air television from 2021 on BBC and ITV |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48388980 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=23 May 2019 |access-date=15 May 2021 |archive-date=15 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515210246/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48388980 |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[Talksport]]'s coverage was presented by [[Mark Saggers]]; [[Sam Matterface]] and [[Andy Townsend]] provided commentary.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Williams|first=Marc|date=15 May 2021|title=FA Cup final live: Chelsea vs Leicester time and how to follow on talkSPORT|url=https://talksport.com/football/fa-cup/869878/fa-cup-final-live-commentary-chelsea-leicester-live-stream-kick-off-time-tv-channel-team-news/|access-date=15 May 2021|publisher=[[Talksport]]|archive-date=14 May 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210514230736/https://talksport.com/football/fa-cup/869878/fa-cup-final-live-commentary-chelsea-leicester-live-stream-kick-off-time-tv-channel-team-news/|url-status=live}}</ref> [[BBC Radio]] also covered the match nationally on [[BBC Radio 5 Live]], whose commentary team comprised [[John Murray (sports broadcaster)|John Murray]] and Dion Dublin,<ref>{{Cite tweet |user=5liveSport |number=1393601058463784965 |title=The commentary team of @bbcjohnmurray & @DionDublinsDube are ready. The 2021 #FACup final.}}</ref> and on the local stations for the two clubs, [[BBC Radio London]] and [[BBC Radio Leicester]].<ref name="talkSPORT">{{cite web |title=FA Cup final: Chelsea v Leicester City |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/m000w8fy |publisher=[[BBC]] |access-date=13 May 2021 |archive-date=13 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210513221411/https://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/m000w8fy |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=BBC Radio Leicester Sport: Commentary, 15/05/2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/p09gtgrq |publisher=[[BBC]] |access-date=13 May 2021 |archive-date=13 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210513221332/https://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/p09gtgrq |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=BBC Radio London Sport, Chelsea v Leicester City |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/p09hbwds |publisher=[[BBC]] |access-date=13 May 2021 |archive-date=13 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210513221409/https://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/p09hbwds |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Opening ceremony===
British singer-songwriter [[Becky Hill]] sang the [[national anthem]] "[[God Save the Queen]]" alongside the [[B Positive Choir]] and the [[Band of the Coldstream Guards]]. The B Positive Choir also sang "[[Abide with Me]]", the traditional FA Cup Final hymn. [[Prince William, Duke of Cambridge]], led the presentation party of FA interim chairman [[Peter McCormick]], [[Emirates (airline)|Emirates]]' UK divisional vice president Richard Jewsbury and chairman of the FA Cup Challenge Committee Steve Curwood.<ref>{{Cite web|title=The Emirates FA Cup Final will celebrate those who helped 2021 competition take place|url=http://www.thefa.com/news/2021/may/10/emirates-fa-cup-thank-you-to-heroes-20210510|access-date=15 May 2021|publisher=[[The Football Association]]|archive-date=15 May 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515121312/https://www.thefa.com/news/2021/may/10/emirates-fa-cup-thank-you-to-heroes-20210510|url-status=live}}</ref> The [[coin flipping|coin toss]] featured a commemorative coin with a red poppy, marking the [[Royal British Legion]]'s centenary.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Troughton |first=Adrian |title=Leicester City and Chelsea captains to use special coin for toss-up at FA Cup final |url=https://www.leicestermercury.co.uk/news/leicester-news/leicester-city-chelsea-captains-use-5398682 |date=11 May 2021 |access-date=15 May 2021 |work=[[Leicester Mercury]] |archive-date=12 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210512112432/https://www.leicestermercury.co.uk/news/leicester-news/leicester-city-chelsea-captains-use-5398682 |url-status=live }}</ref> Players from both teams dropped to one knee immediately prior to kick-off, in support of the No Room For Racism campaign;<ref>{{Cite episode|title=MOTD Live: FA Cup final|series=The FA Cup|network=[[BBC]]|station=[[BBC One HD]]|date=15 May 2021|minutes=61}}</ref> this was booed by some sections of the crowd.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-cup-final-fans-boo-knee-b1848167.html |title=FA Cup final fans boo Chelsea and Leicester players as they take the knee before kick-off |work=[[The Independent]] |date=15 May 2021 |access-date=16 May 2021 |archive-date=15 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515170654/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-cup-final-fans-boo-knee-b1848167.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Match==
===Summary===
====First half====
[[File:Youri Tielemans 2019.jpg|thumb|upright|alt=Youri Tielemans|[[Youri Tielemans]] ''(pictured in 2019)'' scored the only goal of the game.]]

Leicester City [[Kick-off (association football)|kicked off]] the match around 5:15{{nbsp}}p.m. in front of an official attendance of 20,000.<ref name=hr>{{cite web |first1=Conor |first2=Mark |last1=Pope |last2=White |url=https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/live/chelsea-vs-leicester-fa-cup-final-2021 |title=Leicester win their first FA Cup, Live! |work=[[FourFourTwo]] |date=15 May 2021 |access-date=19 May 2021 |archive-date=15 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515174944/https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/live/chelsea-vs-leicester-fa-cup-final-2021 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite web |url=https://www.11v11.com/matches/leicester-city-v-chelsea-15-may-2021-370268/ |website=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |access-date=16 May 2021 |title=Leicester City v Chelsea, 15 May 2021 |archive-date=16 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210516201523/https://www.11v11.com/matches/leicester-city-v-chelsea-15-may-2021-370268/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.chelseafc.com/en/news/2021/05/15/fa-cup-final-report--chelsea-vs-leicester-city|title=FA Cup final report: Chelsea 0 Leicester City 1 | publisher = [[Chelsea F.C.]] |access-date=16 May 2021|archive-date=15 May 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515233255/https://www.chelseafc.com/en/news/2021/05/15/fa-cup-final-report--chelsea-vs-leicester-city|url-status=live}}</ref> Chelsea started the match the dominant side but [[Antonio Rüdiger]]'s 15th minute shot from {{convert|30|yd}} went wide of Leicester City's goal. [[Timothy Castagne]] then sent in a low cross which [[Jamie Vardy]] struck towards the Chelsea goal but the ball was blocked by [[Reece James (footballer, born 1999)|Reece James]]. In the 20th minute, [[Thiago Silva]] was adjudged to have handled the ball after Leicester City's Iheanacho tried to get past him. Tielemans' resulting [[Free kick (association football)|free kick]] was headed over the Chelsea crossbar by [[Çağlar Söyüncü]] from around {{convert|6|yd}}. 
Midway through the first half, rain began to heavily fall.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2021/may/15/chelsea-v-leicester-city-fa-cup-final-live?page=with:block-609ff8cd8f08e659a47e63d9#liveblog-navigation |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=16 May 2021 |date=15 May 2021 |title=Chelsea 0–1 Leicester (3 of 3) |first=Scott |last=Murray }}</ref>

[[Marcos Alonso (footballer, born 1990)|Marcos Alonso]] had a scoring opportunity cleared by Tielemans before Mount's strike from the edge of Leicester City's [[penalty area]] took a deflection from [[Wesley Fofana (footballer)|Wesley Fofana]], going just wide of Leicester City's goal. Werner then struck high over the crossbar from distance before both he and Azpilicueta missed a cross from Mount. Evans became the match's first [[substitute (association football)|substituted]] player when he picked up an injury in the 34th minute and was replaced by Albrighton. A minute later, Fofana was shown the game's first [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow card]] after fouling Werner; Werner was booked minutes later after a lunging slide on Fofana. With three minutes of the half remaining, Pérez was brought down by [[Jorginho (footballer, born December 1991)|Jorginho]] and Söyüncü headed Tielemans' resulting free kick wide from {{convert|12|yd}}. Werner had a shot deflected out for a corner by Fofana. Vardy's stoppage time header from that corner went wide of the Chelsea goal. The half ended 0–0 with neither team registering a shot on target.<ref name=guardasit2>{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2021/may/15/chelsea-v-leicester-city-fa-cup-final-live?page=with:block-60a006238f08e757a7355de4#liveblog-navigation |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=16 May 2021 |date=15 May 2021 |title=Chelsea 0–1 Leicester (2 of 3) |first=Scott |last=Murray }}</ref>

====Second half====
No substitutions were made during the interval. Chelsea kicked off the second half. Eight minutes in, after Leicester City had dominated, Chelsea's [[N'Golo Kanté]] delivered a cross, but Leicester City goalkeeper [[Kasper Schmeichel]] easily caught Alonso's weak header.<ref name=guardasit2/> Schmeichel then saved Azpilicueta's shot. In the 63rd minute, [[Luke Thomas (footballer, born 2001)|Luke Thomas]] passed to Tielemans whose strike from distance flew into the top-left corner of Chelsea's goal, giving Leicester City a 1–0 lead. Despite claims from Chelsea that there had been a handball during the build-up, the goal stood. Chelsea's Ziyech then shot but his strike was deflected out for a corner which came to nothing. Midway through the second half, Iheanacho was replaced by Maddison before Chelsea made a double-substitution, with Ziyech and Alonso coming off for [[Christian Pulisic]] and Chilwell. With 19 minutes remaining, Mount went down after being tackled by Söyüncü but Chelsea's claims for a penalty were denied by the referee.<ref name=guardasit/>

Azpilicueta was then substituted in the 75th minute after a clash of heads. Jorginho was also substituted at the same time, with Havertz and Hudson-Odoi replacing them. Kanté's 78th minute cross was headed goalbound by Chilwell but Schmeichel saved the attempt. With eight minutes of regular time remaining, Chelsea replaced Werner with Giroud; Leicester City's Thomas and Pérez were substituted for [[Hamza Choudhury]] and Morgan. A minute later, Rudiger's shot was high over Leicester City's crossbar before Mount's rising shot was pushed out for a corner by Schmeichel. In the final minute of regular time, Silva passed to Chilwell, who shot; Söyüncü cleared Chilwell's shot off the line but it deflected back off Chilwell and into the Leicester City net. The goal was referred to the VAR, who deemed Chilwell was [[Offside (association football)|offside]] in the build-up and the goal was disallowed. Five minutes of stoppage time were played but there were no more goals. The match ended 1–0 to Leicester City, who won the FA Cup for the first time.<ref name=guardasit>{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2021/may/15/chelsea-v-leicester-city-fa-cup-final-live |title=Chelsea 0–1 Leicester City (1 of 3) |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=16 May 2021 |date=15 May 2021 |first=Scott |last=Murray |archive-date=16 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210516004449/https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2021/may/15/chelsea-v-leicester-city-fa-cup-final-live |url-status=live }}</ref> Tielemans was named [[man of the match]].<ref name="BBC">{{cite web|first=Phil|last=McNulty|authorlink=Phil McNulty|title=Youri Tielemans produced one of the great FA Cup final goals to give Leicester City the trophy for the first time in their history with victory over Chelsea at Wembley|publisher=[[BBC Sport]]|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/57055571|access-date=17 May 2021|date=15 May 2021|archive-date=16 May 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210516212608/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/57055571|url-status=live}}</ref>

